Polchinski's inductive scheme for perturbative renormalization proceeds as follows: one introduces a smooth ultraviolet cutoff in the propagator of a Euclidean quantum field theory, writes down a first-order differential equation (the Polchinski flow equation) for the scale-dependent effective action obtained by integrating out high momenta, and proves perturbative renormalizability by an inductive bound on the scale derivatives of the connected amputated Green functions, organized by the canonical scaling dimension of each interaction vertex. The argument is purely inductive in the renormalization-group scale, avoids explicit Feynman-graph combinatorics, and treats relevant and marginal couplings by integrating the flow equation from the renormalization scale, irrelevant ones from the ultraviolet scale.

Problem. Devise a non-perturbative (constructive) renormalization scheme for Euclidean quantum field theories that
 (i) is fully rigorous, i.e. produces convergent (not merely formal) bounds on the effective action and its functional derivatives uniform in the ultraviolet cutoff, and that yields the Borel summability or analyticity of the Schwinger functions in the coupling constant in a suitable domain;
 (ii) is organized as a single inductive argument on the renormalization-group scale, of comparable conceptual and technical simplicity to Polchinski's perturbative induction (in particular, not relying on cluster or multiscale expansions with explicit tree/forest formulas, large/small field decompositions, or domination procedures specific to each model);
 (iii) applies to fermionic models, where the effective action is a formal power series in anticommuting Grassmann variables and the natural Gaussian "probability" measure is not positive, in such a way that the inductive bounds are obtained directly at the level of the flow equation for the Grassmann effective action.

Either construct such a scheme, or prove that no scheme satisfying (i)–(iii) can exist within a precisely specified framework.

